Title: ✨ feat(gateway): route message gateway connections by platform
URL: <https://github.com/lobehub/lobehub/pull/18628>
Published: N/A
Author: N/A
Highlights:
Adds a second **message gateway host** so a platform's connections can live on a different gateway deployment, chosen by env, with no code change per platform.
...
Today every gateway connection goes to one gateway (`MESSAGE_GATEWAY_URL`, a Cloudflare Worker + Durable Objects). That is the right shape for webhook and hibernation-friendly platforms, but not for platforms whose connection is a permanent long-polling loop (WeChat) or that need native deps a Worker cannot load (Baileys). This PR lets those platforms be served by a long-lived Node container speaking the **same HTTP protocol**, while everything else stays where it is.
...

- `MESSAGE_GATEWAY_NODE_URL` — base URL of the second gateway. Absent → nothing changes, single-host behaviour is byte-for-byte what it was.
- `MESSAGE_GATEWAY_NODE_PLATFORMS` — comma-separated platform ids routed to it (e.g. `wechat`). Everything not listed stays on the default host.
- `resolveMessageGatewayHost(platform)` is the single resolution point; `getMessageGatewayClient(platform)` returns the client for the owning host. Both gateways share `MESSAGE_GATEWAY_SERVICE_TOKEN` — inbound webhook/callback validation only accepts that one value, so a per-gateway token would not isolate anything.
...

### New: messenger polling connections are reconciled

...
`messenger::…` ids were historically excluded from reconciliation — on the default gateway they are durable Durable Objects created lazily at link time, so nothing had to rebuild them. Two things break that assumption:
...

- a container-based gateway keeps its registry in **process memory**, so without a reconcile every redeploy would silently stop all polling until each user re-linked;
- moving a platform between hosts needs the old host's per-user connections torn down and the new host's rebuilt, link by link.
...
`syncMessengerPollingConnections` handles polling-mode messenger platforms only: it first tears down that platform's connections on every _other_ host (a polling platform hosted twice double-delivers every message), then ensure-connects each linked user on the owning host, then disconnects connections whose account link is gone. All three passes reuse the existing blast-radius caps and concurrency limits. Webhook typing shards and websocket singletons stay excluded — they are lazily managed and harmless to lose.
...
- **Routing is env-driven, not capability-driven.** A gateway can report which platforms it _can_ host, but that answers "who is able", while routing needs "who owns" — two gateways both able to host a platform must still not both run it. So ownership stays an explicit env decision.
...
- **Both hosts are always visited by the sync**, even when every platform is routed to one of them. The other still needs its stale cleanup pass, which is what makes rollback self-healing.
...
- **Rollback is asymmetric on purpose**: clear `MESSAGE_GATEWAY_NODE_PLATFORMS` but _keep_ `MESSAGE_GATEWAY_NODE_URL`. Dropping the URL removes the host from the visit list, so nothing would ever tear its side down.
- **Non-goal**: no per-gateway service token, no automatic failover between hosts, no UI. Host membership is an operator decision.
...
, all P
...
. Eleven were confirmed against
...
`syncHost
...
- **A Node-only deployment (no `MESSAGE_GATEWAY_URL`) is out of scope.** Three rounds landed here and each guard moved the breakage rather than removing it. The Node gateway's factory map is `wechat` + `whatsapp-baileys`, so it can never serve the webhook and websocket platforms that fall back to the default host, while gateway mode is one process-wide switch. Supporting it needs a per-platform runtime decision — a design change, not a guard. `isAnyMessageGatewayEnabled()` is therefore anchored on the default host, and `getMessengerWechatConfig` requires both that the runtime will enter gateway mode and that WeChat's owning host is configured, so nothing is advertised that cannot be served.
...
- **A duplicate-delivery window when the SOURCE host's admin surface is unavailable mid-migration** is tracked as LOBE-13379 rather than fixed. Blocking migration connects on an unavailable/incomplete non-owner snapshot would tie the Node gateway's reconcile to the Cloudflare gateway's admin health — the exact coupling this migration removes. Concretely: the current behaviour costs a self-healing duplicate window only when an admin outage overlaps a migration, whereas the blocking rule costs an unrecoverable WeChat outage if that admin is down while the Node gateway restarts. Distinguishing a migration connect from an ordinary one without the source snapshot needs cross-round state, which is the real fix the issue records.
