16: # Codex
17:
18: Codex is OpenAI's coding agent that edits files, runs commands, and ships changes from your terminal. In LobeHub, you can delegate Codex from the desktop app — keep the chat UX you already use, while Codex does the work locally with full access to your project.
19:
20: Send a prompt and Codex opens files, makes edits, runs tests, and reports back. File changes, todos, and command output stream into the chat as the agent moves; sessions resume across turns so a long task can span many messages.
21:
22: ## What Is Codex in LobeHub?
23:
24: A bridge between LobeHub's chat UI and the Codex CLI running on your machine. LobeHub spawns the Codex CLI as a local subprocess, streams its events into a chat conversation, and renders Codex's tool output — file changes, todo lists, command runs — as first-class chat blocks. You drive the agent in natural language; Codex executes locally with your environment, credentials, and project context.
25:
26: ## Requirements
27:
28: - **LobeHub desktop app** — Codex agents only work in the desktop build. The web app cannot spawn local processes.
29: - **Codex CLI installed** — the `codex` command must be available on your `PATH`.
30: - **Signed in** — you must run `codex` once in a terminal to authenticate before LobeHub can drive it.
31:
32: ## Install the Codex CLI
33:
34: Pick one of the install paths:
35:
36: **Recommended (npm)**
37:
38: ```bash
39: npm install -g @openai/codex
40: ```
41:
42: **Homebrew (macOS)**
43:
44: ```bash
45: brew install --cask codex
46: ```
47:
48: After installing, run `codex` once in a terminal to sign in. See the [Codex installation guide](https://github.com/openai/codex#installing-and-running-codex-cli) for details.
49:
50: If LobeHub can't find the CLI, it shows an **Install Codex CLI** prompt with the same commands and an **Open System Tools** button — click it after installing to re-detect the CLI.
51:
52: ## Add Codex in LobeHub
53:
54: When LobeHub detects the Codex CLI on your machine, an **Add Codex** recommendation card appears on the home page tagged "Coding Agent". Click it to create a Codex agent in one step.
55:
56: You can also create one manually from the **Create Agent** menu and pick **Codex** as the type.
57:
58: Each agent is independent, so you can keep multiple Codex agents pinned to different projects or workflows.
59:
60: ## Working Directory
61:
62: Every Codex session is pinned to a working directory — the folder Codex sees as the project root. Set it from the chat input bar before sending your first message. Switching the working directory mid-conversation starts a new Codex session for the topic; chat history stays, but the previous session context cannot be resumed.
63:
64: If you change folders and the saved Codex thread can't be resumed safely, LobeHub shows: *"The saved Codex thread could not be resumed safely, so a new conversation has started for this topic."*
65:
66: ## What Gets Rendered in Chat
67:
68: LobeHub renders Codex's tool calls with purpose-built blocks instead of raw JSON:
69:
70: **File changes** — Codex's edits show up as an expandable list with the operation kind (added, deleted, modified, renamed), the file path, and a per-file line count delta (+/−). Click to see what changed.
71:
72: **Todo lists** — When Codex plans a multi-step task, the plan renders as a progress card with completed / in-progress / pending items and a running count (e.g. "3/5 completed"). Watch tasks tick off as Codex finishes them.
73:
74: **Command execution** — Shell commands Codex runs show the command, exit code, and stdout / stderr output. Success and failure states are clearly marked.
75:
76: **Subagents** — Codex can spawn subagents to work in parallel. Their work appears in isolated threads inside the conversation without leaking into the main bubble.
77:
78: ## Sessions and Resume
79:
80: Codex sessions persist across messages in the same topic. You can send a follow-up like "now also update the tests" and Codex picks up where it left off — same files, same context, same plan.
81:
82: A session can't be resumed if:
83:
84: - The working directory changed since the saved thread was created
85: - The original Codex thread no longer exists
86: - The CLI returns a "no conversation found" or "thread not found" error
87:
88: In any of these cases, LobeHub starts a fresh conversation automatically.
89:
90: ## Where It Can Run
91:
92: The **Execution Device** selector lets you pick where the Codex agent runs:
93:
94: - **This device** — runs Codex as a local process inside the desktop app. Default.
95: - **Cloud sandbox** — runs Codex in an ephemeral cloud sandbox. Useful when you don't want the agent touching your local filesystem.
96: - **Remote device** — drives a remote machine you've connected with `lh connect`. Useful when the project lives on a different machine.
97:
98: ## Limitations
99:
100: - **Desktop only** — the Codex agent runs in the LobeHub desktop app. The web app cannot spawn the CLI.
101: - **One sign-in per machine** — Codex shares its authentication with the global CLI. If `codex` works in your terminal, it works in LobeHub.
102: - **Working-directory-bound** — sessions don't follow you across folders or machines.
103:
104: ## Tips
105:
106: - **Run `codex` once in a terminal first** — sign-in happens at the CLI level, not in LobeHub.
107: - **Pick the working directory before your first message** — switching it later starts a new session.
108: - **Watch the todo card** — it's the fastest read on what Codex thinks it still has to do.
109: - **Use one Codex agent per project** — pinning each agent to a specific repo keeps sessions tidy and resumable.
